I have an array of strings:
["A", "a", "B", "b"]
I want to sort them case-sensitively, so that the result is ["A", "B", "a", "b"].
I know that in JS, the default sort is case-insensitive. So I need to write a comparator function. But how?
I tried the following:
arr.sort(function(a, b) {
    if (a < b) return -1;
    if (a > b) return 1;
    return 0;
});
But this gives me ["A", "a", "B", "b"], which is not what I want. So the problem is that the default sort is case-insensitive. But how to make it case-sensitive?
I found that the default sort is case-insensitive, but when I use the comparator function, it is case-sensitive. But in this case, the comparator function is not working as expected. What is the problem here?
